<p>Some shops and restaurant menus use the term <strong>A5+ Wagyu</strong>. Visually, it suggests a category above A5, as though the plus sign represented an official sixth level. In fact, <strong>the official Japanese grading system does not include an A5+ grade</strong>: A5 is already the highest combination of yield grade A and quality grade 5.</p>
<p>Why, then, is A5+ used? Usually as commercial shorthand to distinguish A5 beef that a seller considers exceptionally marbled or that falls within certain high BMS values. The problem begins when the plus sign is presented as though it were a separate official Japanese grade.</p>
<h2>How the Japanese grading system actually works</h2>
<p>Japanese beef carcass grading combines two elements. The <strong>yield grade</strong> is expressed as A, B or C. The <strong>quality grade</strong> runs from 1 to 5.</p>
<p>Combining them creates grades such as A3, A4 and A5. A5 therefore means yield grade A and quality grade 5. There is no official A6 or A5+ grade issued by the Japan Meat Grading Association.</p>
<p>For the full basics, see <a href="/en/japanese-wagyu-a5-what-it-is-what-it-means/">what A5 Wagyu means</a>.</p>
<h2>So what does BMS measure?</h2>
<p>BMS, or Beef Marbling Standard, is used to evaluate marbling. The scale runs from 1 to 12. Within the official system, the marbling range associated with quality grade 5 is <strong>BMS 8–12</strong>.</p>
<p>This means two carcasses can both be A5 while still having different marbling levels within the quality-5 range. One may be BMS 8 and another BMS 12.</p>
<p>This is where some sellers use expressions such as A5+, A5 BMS 10+ or A5 BMS 12 to communicate that a product sits toward the upper end of the marbling range.</p>
<h2>A5+ can mean different things to different sellers</h2>
<p>Because A5+ is not an official standardised grade, <strong>there is no universal definition</strong>. One retailer may use it for BMS 10–12, another only for BMS 12, and another simply as shorthand for “specially selected A5.”</p>
<p>The plus sign therefore tells you little unless the seller defines it. The useful question is: <strong>what exact criterion does this seller use to call the beef A5+?</strong></p>
<h2>Is A5 BMS 12 officially above A5 BMS 8?</h2>
<p>Both can share the A5 grade because both fall within quality grade 5 and meet the other criteria required for that quality level. BMS 12, however, indicates a higher marbling score than BMS 8.</p>
<p>That does not create a new official grade above A5. It simply adds more precise information about one evaluated factor.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/en/wagyu-bms-marbling-scale-1-12/">BMS guide</a> explains why the number should not be treated as a total flavour score.</p>
<h2>Higher BMS does not automatically mean better for everyone</h2>
<p>Maximum marbling can be spectacular, but it also creates a much richer and fattier eating experience. Some consumers prefer BMS 8 or 9 because they perceive a stronger balance between muscle and fat.</p>
<p>A BMS 12 cut can be ideal for a tasting built around a few bites and still feel excessive to someone expecting to eat a large steak.</p>
<p>For that reason, <strong>more does not always mean better for you</strong>. Cut, portion and occasion matter just as much.</p>
<h2>A5 does not mean every cut looks identical</h2>
<p>The carcass is graded at a standard evaluation point. That does not mean every muscle from the animal has exactly the same marbling pattern.</p>
<p>Ribeye, striploin and tenderloin from the same animal can show different fat distribution and produce different eating experiences.</p>
<p>See <a href="/en/wagyu-ribeye-striploin-tenderloin-differences/">Wagyu ribeye, striploin or tenderloin</a> if you are choosing a cut.</p>
<h2>What should a serious product page show?</h2>
<p>If a retailer uses A5+, it should support the claim with objective information: country of origin, breed where relevant, official A5 grade, BMS where available, cut, weight and traceability.</p>
<p>A listing that only says “premium A5+” without origin or documentation sounds impressive but gives the buyer little basis for comparison.</p>
<h2>Can A5+ be useful commercial shorthand?</h2>
<p>Yes, provided it is defined. A retailer might, for example, explain that it internally uses “A5+” for A5 cuts with BMS 10–12. In that context the expression works as the retailer’s own selection category.</p>
<p>The important point is <strong>not to confuse that internal selection with a grade issued by the official Japanese grading body</strong>.</p>
<h2>What should you ask before buying A5+?</h2>
<p>Ask for the official grade, declared BMS, country and region of origin, the exact cut and available traceability documentation. Those details allow much cleaner product comparisons.</p>
<p>See <a href="/en/how-to-tell-authentic-japanese-wagyu-certification-traceability/">how to identify authentic Japanese Wagyu</a> for the full purchasing checklist.</p>
<h2>The conclusion</h2>
<p><strong>A5+ is not an official grade above A5.</strong> The Japanese system tops out at A5. Within quality grade 5, marbling can range from BMS 8 to 12, and some sellers use the plus sign to highlight beef toward the top of that range.</p>
<p>The term does not have to be misleading if it is clearly defined, but it should never replace objective data. When you see A5+, look for BMS, origin, cut and traceability.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Does official A5+ Wagyu exist in Japan?</h3>
<p>Not as a separate official grade. The highest official grade is A5.</p>
<h3>What BMS does A5 Wagyu have?</h3>
<p>For the marbling criterion to correspond to quality grade 5, BMS falls within 8–12, while the other quality criteria must also be satisfied.</p>
<h3>Is A5 BMS 12 better than A5 BMS 8?</h3>
<p>It has more marbling, but that does not guarantee you will prefer it. BMS measures marbling rather than universal eating preference.</p>
<h3>Should I avoid a retailer that uses A5+?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. What matters is whether the retailer defines the plus sign and provides objective information on grade, BMS, origin and traceability.</p>
